# Mutation cases for the member surface, one language over.
#
# Run with
# `scripts/mutate sce-build/tests/mutations/the_frontend_now_owns_the_member_surface.cases`.
#
# Five cases stood in `lua_engine_reads_ecmascript.cases` aimed at
# `EcmaScriptToLuaTransformer`. On 2026-08-29 that rewriter was RETIRED: the
# engine lowers the author's ECMAScript with `sce-build`'s frontend and refuses
# what the frontend refuses, which the seam document's `retire-rewriter` row
# asserts and `the_rewriter_is_retired_not_merely_second.cases` mutates. Nothing
# reaches the code those cases broke, so every one of them would have reported
# SURVIVED — and none of them had stopped being a defect.
#
# That casefile's own header had already named this failure twice, on the same
# day, for the same reason: *"a seam that takes work away from a component
# takes the corpus's grip on it at the same moment, silently, and only a full
# round says so."* The retirement takes the last of it. The distinguishing
# question stays "who answers this now", and the answer for every clause below
# is `sce-build/src/ecmascript/lua.rs`.
#
# What each case breaks is the clause it always broke:
#
#   * §13-3-3 an Array is a 1-based Lua sequence, so a numeric index moves by
#     one. Leaving it alone answers a NEIGHBOURING element — a wrong value, not
#     an error.
#   * §15-4-4-4 `concat` appends every argument. The shared `_concat` takes one
#     other value, so the arguments are FOLDED through it; passing them to a
#     single call drops all but the first and answers a shorter array. The
#     rewriter did exactly that, which is why the fold is spelled out.
#   * §15-4-4-7 `push` answers the new length. `table.insert` answers nothing,
#     so the receiver is held in a local and the length returned.
#   * §15-4-4 / §15-5-4 a member method is implemented by the shared
#     `ecma_semantics.lua` definition, not by Lua's own library. `table.sort`
#     is the habit Lua makes available and it orders numbers numerically where
#     ECMAScript orders them as strings.
#
# ⚠ **A fifth case did NOT come with them, and is retired rather than moved.**
# It broke the placeholder that made a string literal invisible as a receiver
# to every backward-scanning pass, so `'abc'.length` reached Lua as itself. A
# parser has no backward scan and no placeholder: a literal receiver is a
# subtree, and the defect class cannot be constructed here. Retiring it is the
# honest answer; inventing a frontend mutation that produces the same wrong
# text would be a case about nothing. The property itself is still asked —
# `ecmascript_member_access.rs` and the shared table both put a literal in
# receiver position.

mutation_tests -p sce-build --features cli --test ecmascript_semantics
mutation_targets sce-build/src/ecmascript/lua.rs
mutation_oracles sce-build/tests/ecmascript_semantics.rs

mutation_case "a numeric index is read one element short" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/src/ecmascript/lua.rs",
     'return Ok(format!("{}[{}]", operand(object)?, n + 1));',
     'return Ok(format!("{}[{}]", operand(object)?, n));')
PY

mutation_case "concat keeps only its first argument" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/src/ecmascript/lua.rs",
     """                let mut folded = receiver;
                for arg in emitted {
                    folded = format!("_concat({folded}, {arg})");
                }""",
     """                let mut folded = receiver;
                for arg in emitted {
                    folded = format!("_concat({folded}, {arg})");
                    break;
                }""")
PY

mutation_case "push answers nothing where the length was promised" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/src/ecmascript/lua.rs",
     '"(function() local __t = {receiver} {inserts}return #__t end)()"',
     '"(function() local __t = {receiver} {inserts}end)()"')
PY

mutation_case "sort loses the shared definition that implements it" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/src/ecmascript/lua.rs",
     '                    "sort" => "_scxml_sort",',
     '                    "sort" => "table.sort",')
PY
